# Website/ → Vercel Project: picklepro-homepage

## HARD RULE

Whenever you update any file inside the `Website/` folder and the user asks to commit, push, or deploy, you **must** deploy to the Vercel project below — never to any other project.

## Correct project

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Project name | `picklepro-homepage` |
| Project ID | `prj_8M9pmkeVs9nNtUNJkdRsqZKqvp30` |
| Org / team ID | `team_wiF9YAYMy3tvn0tO3a3UBcsf` |
| Production URL | `academypro.thecourtflow.com` |
| Preview URL pattern | `picklepro-homepage-*.vercel.app` |

## Deploy command (always run from inside Website/)

```bash
cd Website && VERCEL_PROJECT_ID=prj_8M9pmkeVs9nNtUNJkdRsqZKqvp30 VERCEL_ORG_ID=team_wiF9YAYMy3tvn0tO3a3UBcsf vercel deploy --prod --yes
```

The `VERCEL_PROJECT_ID` / `VERCEL_ORG_ID` env vars are **required** because the repo root has a separate `.vercel/project.json` pointing to a different project (`pickleball-hero`). Without them the CLI will pick up the wrong project.

The `Website/.vercel/project.json` also encodes the correct project but the env vars are the reliable override.

## Do NOT

- Run `vercel deploy` from the repo root (deploys the wrong project).
- Run `vercel deploy` without the env vars from any subdirectory (CLI walks up and finds the wrong root `.vercel`).
- Deploy `Website/` to `pickleball-hero` (`prj_iNrUHlh38vtRwgbmAKheWfwDVjTR`).
